WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.800
The Hubble Space Telescope has recorded a 10-sided cloud pattern circling the South Pole of Saturn, in a formation that contrasts sharply with the planet's usual atmospheric bands. This is Fascinating Frontiers, episode 187. World leaders, along with ministers, astronauts, and industry figures, are assembling in Paris for the International Space Summit. Joseph Ashbacher, who leads the European Space Agency, intends to lay out a long-term plan for the continent's activities

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:46.180
beyond our planet. The presentation will cover what part Europe aims to take in operations ranging from low-Earth orbit, out to the moon, and onward to Mars. Attendees will review shared priorities and possible joint steps among participating nations and companies.

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:59.180
Sedimentary rock layers in California's Soledad Basin tilt at sharp angles after millions of years of deformation. The dramatic formations have served as filming locations for Star Trek and other productions.

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:12.860
Source material notes the tilted strata as evidence of ancient basin processes recorded by NASA observations. The site preserves clear examples of how tectonic forces reshape surface geology over deep time.

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:23.120
Researchers continue to study the layers for insights into regionally deformation history. The outcrops remain accessible for both scientific field work and public visits.

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:38.820
Dr. Iman Jahangir flew on a blue-origin sub-orbital mission in August 2024, becoming the first space-faring commercial cardiologist. His new book details the medical and personal steps that led an Iranian-American physician to the Carmen line.

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:49.460
The flight provided direct experience of sub-orbital conditions and their physiological effects. Jahangir documented cardiovascular responses during the brief weightless interval.

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:58.960
The account combines clinical data with the sequence of training and selection that preceded launch. Publication of the book makes those records available for wider review.

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:13.280
From Europe's strategic planning, we turn to a concrete mission already nearing its destination. The joint ESA, Jaxas spacecraft, known as Bepi Colombo, is completing its final approach to mercury after an eight-year journey.

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:21.420
Two separate orbiters on board will examine the surface makeup, the magnetic field, and the thin exosphere once they reach the target.

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:30.420
Measurements are expected to shed light on why the planet carries an oversized core and what that reveals about conditions in the early solar system.

00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:36.920
After a series of flybyes, the two orbiters will separate and settle into different paths around the planet.

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:48.000
Information on how the exosphere interacts with the solar wind should help explain the large core fraction and how heat and magnetic activity evolved on terrestrial worlds.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:57.580
Mission teams will also look for signs of ancient volcanic activity that might connect mercury's surface history to the first epochs of the inner solar system.

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:03.960
While one mission closes in on an inner planet, another collaboration is just beginning to take shape.

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:09.760
Europe and India are moving forward with talks on joint technology work and future mission ideas.

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:18.000
The conversations center on satellite systems and deep space research projects that draw on strengths already present in each program.

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:25.340
Shared development efforts could open new opportunities for both sides in areas where their capabilities complement each other.

00:03:26.020 --> 00:03:32.460
A new electromagnetic launch system under development would accelerate small satellites to orbital velocity from the ground.

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:38.080
The approach seeks to reduce the cost and complexity of reaching space for certain payloads.

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:44.260
Engineers are testing rail and coil configurations capable of imparting the required velocity increments.

00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:49.720
Ground-based acceleration removes the need for onboard propellant in the initial ascent phase.

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:54.660
The design targets small satellite classes that can tolerate the high G environment.

00:03:55.380 --> 00:03:59.980
Development timelines remain tied to component durability and power delivery milestones.

00:04:00.980 --> 00:04:08.260
On September 8th, the moon will pass in front of Jupiter before dawn, hiding the planet from view in a rare daytime occultation.

00:04:09.020 --> 00:04:13.680
Observers in parts of North America can watch the event with binoculars or small telescopes.

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:19.800
The geometry places the disappearance and reappearance phases during daylight hours for many locations.

00:04:20.580 --> 00:04:25.360
Timing predictions rely on updated lunar ephemeralides and Jupiter's current position.

00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:31.480
The event offers a chance to observe the relative motions of the two bodies against the daytime sky.

00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:36.260
Clear horizons and modest optical aid are the main requirements for viewing.

00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:44.100
Researchers developed a framework to estimate economic losses from severe space weather events affecting the US electricity network.

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:48.960
The model incorporates historical storm data and grid vulnerability factors.

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:55.060
Input parameters include storm intensity distributions drawn from past geomagnetic records.

00:04:55.680 --> 00:05:00.920
Output maps show projected impacts on transmission and generation assets under different scenarios.

00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:06.480
The framework allows sensitivity testing of mitigation measures such as transformer hardening.

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:12.400
Validation draws on documented effects from earlier events that reached comparable disturbance levels.

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:27.020
Observations of transitional millisecond pulsar PSR Joan 1227 to 4853 recorded hundreds of ultra short high energy pulses over 174 hours of radio data.

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:31.580
In one hour alone the source produced 114 giant pulses.

00:05:32.260 --> 00:05:36.360
The extreme behavior links to open questions about pulsar emission physics.

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:41.980
Data collection spanned multiple sessions to capture variability in pulse amplitude and timing.

00:05:42.540 --> 00:05:48.380
The transitional nature of the system allows comparison between accretion powered and rotation powered states.

00:05:48.980 --> 00:05:54.060
Continued monitoring aims to refine models of how the emission mechanism switches between regimes.

00:05:54.740 --> 00:06:00.760
When merging black holes settle into a final state they emit a fading ring down signal in gravitational waves.

00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:07.920
Researchers are exploring whether precise measurements of that signal could reveal deviations from the simple no-hair theorem.

00:06:08.540 --> 00:06:13.260
The approach focuses on the frequency and damping times of the dominant ring down modes.

00:06:13.940 --> 00:06:19.080
Current detectors provide the sensitivity needed to test for small departures in the final wave form.

00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:24.520
Simulated signals help quantify how much deviation would be detectable above noise.

00:06:24.520 --> 00:06:31.020
The method offers one route to probe whether the end state matches the predictions of classical general relativity.

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:35.480
Partnerships on Earth set the stage for new views of familiar worlds.

00:06:35.980 --> 00:06:41.720
The Hubble Space Telescope captured a rare 10-sided cloud structure rotating around Saturn's south pole.

00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:48.620
The pattern appears distinct from the planet's typical east-west cloud bands that dominate most of its visible atmosphere.

00:06:49.240 --> 00:06:55.080
Repeated imaging shows the formation holding its shape while the rest of the atmosphere continues its usual motion.

00:06:55.600 --> 00:07:00.720
From Saturn's striking clouds we shift to a fresh look at one of the most studied patches of sky.

00:07:01.380 --> 00:07:08.500
The NSF DOE Vera C. Ruben Observatory has produced an exceptionally deep image of the well-known cosmos field.

00:07:09.040 --> 00:07:16.620
The new view reaches fainter objects than previous surveys of the same region and will feed studies of how galaxies change over time.

00:07:16.620 --> 00:07:24.100
Early data from the facility already demonstrate its ability to deliver the depth needed for such long-term cosmic investigations.

00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:30.240
While large surveys map galaxies, precise stellar measurements are revealing hidden black holes.

00:07:30.900 --> 00:07:36.840
Data from the Gaia mission have identified three stellar mass black holes, each paired with a small companion star.

00:07:37.400 --> 00:07:42.900
In two of the systems the companion orbits at a distance tighter than current formation models normally allow.

00:07:42.900 --> 00:07:50.040
The closed separations raise questions about how such pairs remained bound through the stages that usually disrupt binaries.

00:07:50.680 --> 00:07:56.260
From quiet black holes we move to a proposed mechanism that may have helped spark life on early Earth.

00:07:56.840 --> 00:08:04.120
Researchers have examined whether powerful ion beams tied to ancient auroras could have concentrated energy in Earth's early atmosphere.

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:10.900
Laboratory work indicates these beams can generate organic molecules at rates far above what steady sunlight achieves

00:08:11.220 --> 00:08:14.900
because the energy arrives in repeated intense pulses.

00:08:15.540 --> 00:08:25.060
The beams would have operated between 80 and 150 kilometers altitude where a charged particles break apart neutral molecules and send reactive fragments downward.

00:08:25.720 --> 00:08:33.220
The process tends to narrow as ionization increases, focusing its effect in a manner similar to a natural particle accelerator.

00:08:33.220 --> 00:08:42.640
One remaining uncertainty is whether the beams persisted long enough or across enough of the planet's surface to influence the rise of self-replicating chemistry.

00:08:43.260 --> 00:08:46.800
From deep time on Earth we return to near-atarm mission updates.

00:08:47.500 --> 00:08:54.000
The China National Space Administration announced that the Cheng E7 mission missed its late August launch window.

00:08:54.520 --> 00:09:01.640
The postponement affects the next step in China's robotic lunar exploration sequence but does not alter longer term plans.

00:09:01.640 --> 00:09:07.860
Mission planners are evaluated revised windows while hardware remains in storage or test configurations.

00:09:08.480 --> 00:09:13.040
The sequence includes sample return and in situ resource demonstration elements.

00:09:13.720 --> 00:09:17.800
Updated schedules will be released once technical and range constraints are resolved.

00:09:18.440 --> 00:09:22.880
International partners continue to track the adjusted timeline for coordinated observations.

00:09:23.520 --> 00:09:31.860
Before we go, keep an eye on the next steps for the delayed Cheng E7 lunar mission and any new details that emerge on its revised timeline.

